# Python Development with Virtual Environment

## Introduction

This rule defines standards for Python development using virtual environments (`.venv`). It covers project structure, dependency management, coding conventions, testing, and best practices to maintain clean, maintainable, and production-ready Python code.

## Project Structure

Follow this recommended project structure for Python applications:

```
project-root/
├── .venv/                    # Virtual environment (gitignored)
├── src/
│   └── your_package/
│       ├── __init__.py
│       ├── main.py
│       ├── core/
│       │   ├── __init__.py
│       │   ├── models.py
│       │   └── services.py
│       ├── api/
│       │   ├── __init__.py
│       │   ├── routes.py
│       │   └── middleware.py
│       └── utils/
│           ├── __init__.py
│           ├── helpers.py
│           └── validators.py
├── tests/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── conftest.py
│   ├── test_core/
│   ├── test_api/
│   └── test_utils/
├── docs/
│   ├── README.md
│   └── api.md
├── scripts/
│   ├── setup.sh
│   └── deploy.sh
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
├── pyproject.toml
├── requirements.txt
├── requirements-dev.txt
└── README.md
```

## Virtual Environment Setup

### Creating and Activating Virtual Environment

```bash
# Create virtual environment
python -m venv .venv

# Activate on Windows
.venv\Scripts\activate

# Activate on macOS/Linux
source .venv/bin/activate

# Verify activation
which python  # Should point to .venv/bin/python
pip list      # Should show only base packages
```

